"# Chapter 7 — The fold — one program, many small pieces

Big programs in Motoi are not built by growing one file to a thousand tangled lines. They are built by combining many small pieces — helper verbs, each with one job — and *folding* them together. The other dialect calls these pieces \"modules\"; in Motoi they are just top-level `define`s that call the already-loaded library. The idea of the fold is dialect-independent, and it is one of the most important habits you can build.

## 7.1 What the fold looks like

Here is a tiny \"frame loop\" folded out of three helpers — one for input, one for drawing, one for sound. In another Scheme each might be its own imported module; in Motoi they are three `define`s, and the library verbs they call (`string-append`, `list`, `when`) were loaded at boot:

```scheme
(define (read-keys) (list 'space))                 ; stands in for input
(define (draw where) (string-append \"drew \" where)) ; stands in for graphics
(define (play sound) (string-append \"played \" sound))

(define (frame)
  (let ((keys (read-keys)))
    (list (if (memq 'space keys) (play \"jump\") \"silent\")
          (draw \"player\"))))

(frame)
;; ⇒ (\"played jump\" \"drew player\")
```

The `frame` verb is short. Almost everything it does is delegated to a helper. Its own job is thin: call the pieces in a particular order and fold the results together. That is the fold.

## 7.2 Why the fold works

Because each piece has one job, each piece is small enough to understand. `draw` does not need to know about sound. `play` does not need to know about input. Only `frame` knows about all three, and its knowledge is thin — an order of calls.

When something goes wrong, you isolate it. Sound is silent? Look at `play` alone. Sprite invisible? Look at `draw` alone. You do not have to hold the whole program in your head at once.

## 7.3 The fold vs the pile

The opposite of the fold is the pile: one verb a thousand lines long, doing everything inline, sharing no structure. Piles are easier to write on day one and harder to work with on every day after.

Programs drift toward the pile by default, because the pile is what you get when you keep adding lines without pausing to ask *should this be its own verb?* The habit to build: whenever a chunk of logic has its own clear job, cut it into a named helper. Even a two-line helper is worth extracting — it can be reused and tested on its own.

## 7.4 A fold with setup

Pieces often need setup — a table precomputed, a value established — before the loop runs. The fold spells the setup order out explicitly, as plain calls in sequence:

```scheme
(define (init-input) 'input-ready)
(define (init-graphics w h) (list 'graphics w h))
(define (init-sound channels) (list 'sound channels))

(define (setup)
  (list (init-input)
        (init-graphics 800 600)
        (init-sound 8)))

(setup)
;; ⇒ (input-ready (graphics 800 600) (sound 8))
```

If order matters — say, sound must be set up before graphics — the fold makes that dependency *visible* as a sequence, instead of hiding it inside one giant verb.

## 7.5 A fold with teardown

Symmetrically, pieces often need cleanup, and the common pattern is to tear down in reverse of setup order. Motoi's `dynamic-wind` handles guaranteed cleanup around a body:

```scheme
(define (run-with-cleanup)
  (dynamic-wind
    (lambda () 'set-up)
    (lambda () (frame))
    (lambda () 'torn-down)))

(run-with-cleanup)
;; ⇒ (\"played jump\" \"drew player\")
```

`dynamic-wind` runs the first thunk (setup), then the body, then the last thunk (teardown) — even if the body errors. Real cleanup would close files or drop connections in reverse init order; the shape is the same.

## 7.6 Sharing state — through a mediator, not a global

Pieces should not reach into each other's private state. If two helpers both need a shared value, one *owns* it and the other receives it — the top-level verb acts as mediator, passing the value explicitly:

```scheme
(define (make-window w h) (list 'window w h))
(define (input-using window) (list 'input-bound-to window))

(define (wire-up)
  (let ((win (make-window 800 600)))   ; graphics owns the window
    (input-using win)))                ; input receives a reference

(wire-up)
;; ⇒ (input-bound-to (window 800 600))
```

Passing the window explicitly is more verbose than a shared global — deliberately so. The verbosity makes the dependency visible. Swap in a different window later and you know exactly what `input-using` needs.

## 7.7 The fold is a spec

The top of a well-folded program — the `setup`, the small `main`, the order of calls — is, in a real sense, a specification of what the program is made of. Read the top and you know the shape, not from comments but from structure. That is a lot to learn from a few lines, and it is why the fold matters more than it looks like it should.

## 7.8 Try it

Take a verb you have written that has grown long. Look for a chunk with its own clear job — anything general-purpose, anything not tied to this verb's one purpose. Cut it into a named helper and call it back. Do it again with a second chunk. Watch the original verb shrink into a fold: a short sequence of calls, most of the work delegated to small, testable helpers.

Chapter 8 covers, in the other dialect, how modules span multiple carts — and, for Motoi, what \"sharing code across carts\" really means when the library is already everywhere. Turn the page.")
